config.json
# Timestamped/editor backups of config.json carry the same populated API keys as
# config.json itself. Only the exact name was ignored, so a `git add -A` in a
# checkout holding one would have staged live credentials into a public repo.
config.json.bak*
config.json.*.bak
config.json.orig
config.json.save
config.json~
# The .env that now holds the third-party integration keys, same reasoning.
# config.json is not deprecated -- it keeps the other 35 settings. hate_crack *creates*
# this file at first startup (from a legacy config.json, or from defaults), so a
# populated one exists in the checkout of anyone who has run the tool, and it can
# hold up to four secrets: HASHVIEW_API_KEY, HASHMOB_API_KEY,
# NOTIFY_PUSHOVER_TOKEN and NOTIFY_PUSHOVER_USER. The backup spellings are listed
# for the same reason they are above: ignoring only the exact name is what let a
# backup slip through last time. `.env.example` is deliberately NOT ignored -- it
# is tracked, generated from schema defaults, and holds no values.
.env
.env.bak*
.env.*.bak
.env.orig
.env.save
.env~
# Playwright MCP scratch output (console logs, traces, screenshots).
.playwright-mcp/
# hcatPotfilePath falls back to "hashcat.potfile" inside the checkout, so the
# name hate_crack itself writes needs covering too, not just hashcat's default.
#
# Both spellings are globbed rather than named, because hcatPotfilePath is a
# configurable setting: point it at engagement.potfile or client.pot and the two
# literal names that used to be listed here matched nothing. A potfile is a
# hash:plaintext list, so that was the same exposure as the artifacts below with
# a different filename. SECRET_ARTIFACT_PATTERNS in tests/test_repo_hygiene.py
# has always used these globs; .gitignore was the narrower of the two.
*.pot
*.potfile
# Cracked-plaintext session artifacts. `.out` is a hash:plaintext list and
# `.passwords` is pure plaintext; the rest carry hashes or intermediate cracks.
# These were ignored only by .git/info/exclude, which is local-only and does not
# clone, so on any other contributor's machine `git add -A` staged them into a
# public repo. No tracked file matches any of these patterns.
*.out
*.passwords
*.working
*.combined
*.nt
*.lm
*.cracked
*.xlsx
# The *source* dump, which the patterns above missed. Everything derived from an
# NTDS extract was covered -- `corp.ntds.nt`, `corp.ntds.nt.out` -- but
# `corp.ntds` and `ntds.dit` themselves were not, and a raw NTDS extract is a
# domain-wide hash dump: the single highest-value artifact in this workflow.
# Locally it was covered only by a `*ntds*` line in .git/info/exclude, which
# does not clone, so every other contributor's checkout staged it on `git add
# -A` -- the same gap that the block above exists to close, one step upstream.
# Deliberately `*.ntds` and not `*ntds*`: the broad form would swallow tracked
# files such as tests/test_upload_cracked_hashes.py.
*.ntds
ntds.dit
# Hash lists written with an explicit extension. Narrow on purpose, for the same
# reason: `*hashes*` matches a tracked test file.
*.hashes
# Per-attack scratch directories (basewords, candidate lists, LLM patterns),
# removed by cleanup() but left behind by a crash or a hard interrupt.
*.rosetta/
*.spoonman/
*.llm_patterns/
# hashcat --debug-mode 5 output. hate_crack writes one of these for every
# rule-based attack without being asked (see _add_debug_mode_for_rules), and
# each line pairs a rule with the plaintext it cracked. The default
# hcatDebugLogPath is the relative "./hashcat_debug", so on any run launched
# from a checkout they land in the repo. The second pattern catches the same
# logs when hcatDebugLogPath points somewhere else inside the tree.
hashcat_debug/
hashcat_debug*.log
# Operator wordlist collections, same relative-default reasoning as
# hashcat_debug/ above: config.json.example ships hcatWordlists as
# "./wordlists" and hcatOptimizedWordlists as "./optimized_wordlists", so on a
# checkout that kept the defaults the corpora land in the repo. They are also
# large enough to matter -- a real collection runs to tens of gigabytes -- and
# a cracked-password corpus is client data, which is the one thing that must
# never reach a public remote. Untracked content here also blocks the
# `git status --porcelain` dirty check in _run_upgrade(), so an operator using
# the shipped defaults cannot self-upgrade until it is ignored. No tracked file
# matches either pattern.
wordlists/
optimized_wordlists/
hate_crack/__init__.pyc
hate_crack/_version.py
__pycache__/
hate_crack/__pycache__/
tests/__pycache__/
uv.lock
build/
dist/
hate_crack.egg-info/
hate_crack/hashcat-utils/
hate_crack/princeprocessor/
*.txt.expanded
*.hcmask
*.ollama_candidates
*.filtered
research/
--help
4_char_all
all_hashes.enabled
some_histories

# Local agent tooling and instructions - intentionally not published.
#
# CLAUDE.md and .claude/ used to live here. They are published deliberately as
# of 2026-08-21: CLAUDE.md is the contributor-facing account of the branching,
# release and config policies, and .claude/ holds the tooling it refers to.
# Only the personal and the genuinely local remain unpublished.
#
# settings.local.json is per-developer, per-machine permission state. Claude
# Code's own docs call it "personal/not checked in" and auto-add it to global
# git excludes; committing it would also gate its rules behind the workspace
# trust dialog, which is not what a personal file is for.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/plans/
.claude/specs/
docs/plans/
docs/superpowers/

#possible client data
left_*
found_*
